      <!-- SECTION 5: Commercial Hauling & Dump Truck Logistics -->
      <section id="hauling-logistics" class="bg-white p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-2xs space-y-5 scroll-mt-20">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-100 pb-4">
          <span class="text-[11px] font-bold text-emerald-700 bg-emerald-50 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md uppercase tracking-wider">Logistics & Jobsite Delivery</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mt-2">Dump Truck Hauling & Commercial Delivery Logistics</h2>
        </div>

        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          Ordering bulk materials requires understanding highway weight regulations and truck axle physics. In the United States, commercial dump truck loads are strictly capped by <strong>State Department of Transportation (DOT) Gross Vehicle Weight Ratings (GVWR)</strong> and the <strong>Federal Bridge Formula</strong>. Even if a dump body has volumetric space remaining, dense materials (stone, wet soil, concrete) hit weight limits long before the bed is filled.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-3.5">
          <div class="p-4 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 space-y-1.5">
            <span class="text-[10px] font-mono font-bold bg-slate-200 text-slate-800 px-2 py-0.5 rounded uppercase">Class 6/7 Chassis</span>
            <div class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Single-Axle Dump</div>
            <div class="text-emerald-700 font-mono font-bold text-sm">5 to 7 cu yds</div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600">Payload limit: <strong>7 to 8 US tons</strong>. Ideal for tight residential driveways, alleys, and smaller renovations.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 space-y-1.5">
            <span class="text-[10px] font-mono font-bold bg-slate-200 text-slate-800 px-2 py-0.5 rounded uppercase">Class 8 10-Wheeler</span>
            <div class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Tandem-Axle Dump</div>
            <div class="text-emerald-700 font-mono font-bold text-sm">10 to 12 cu yds</div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600">Payload limit: <strong>13 to 15 US tons</strong>. The standard workhorse commercial hauling truck across American quarries.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 space-y-1.5">
            <span class="text-[10px] font-mono font-bold bg-slate-200 text-slate-800 px-2 py-0.5 rounded uppercase">Lift Axle Chassis</span>
            <div class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Tri-Axle Dump</div>
            <div class="text-emerald-700 font-mono font-bold text-sm">14 to 16 cu yds</div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600">Payload limit: <strong>18 to 22 US tons</strong>. Highest payload efficiency for major site preparation and roadway base.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 space-y-1.5">
            <span class="text-[10px] font-mono font-bold bg-slate-200 text-slate-800 px-2 py-0.5 rounded uppercase">Trailer / Semi</span>
            <div class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Super Dump / End Dump</div>
            <div class="text-emerald-700 font-mono font-bold text-sm">18 to 22 cu yds</div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600">Payload limit: <strong>24 to 26 US tons</strong>. Bulk deliveries requiring vast turnaround access and wide turning radius.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 pt-2">Pre-Delivery Jobsite Clearance Checklist</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          Before scheduling a 60,000-lb commercial truck delivery, verify the following structural site parameters to prevent property damage and driver drop refusals:
        </p>

        <ul class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs text-slate-700">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 bg-slate-50 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-200">
            <span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold shrink-0">✓</span>
            <div><strong>Vertical Overhead Clearance:</strong> Requires 14 feet clear driving height and 20 to 24 feet vertical clearance with dump bed fully elevated. Inspect power drops, cable lines, and overhanging tree limbs.</div>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 bg-slate-50 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-200">
            <span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold shrink-0">✓</span>
            <div><strong>Underground Utility & Septic Setback:</strong> Never permit tandem or tri-axle trucks to drive across septic tanks, drainage fields, water meters, or shallow irrigation manifolds. Keep 20 ft minimum separation.</div>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 bg-slate-50 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-200">
            <span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold shrink-0">✓</span>
            <div><strong>Driveway Surface Integrity:</strong> Standard residential concrete driveways (3.5" unreinforced) fracture under the axle weight of a fully loaded tri-axle. Stage deliveries on the street curb or lay double-thick 3/4" plywood tracks.</div>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 bg-slate-50 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-200">
            <span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold shrink-0">✓</span>
            <div><strong>Slope & Tipping Hazards:</strong> Dump beds raised 20 feet shift the truck's center of gravity drastically. Drivers will not elevate beds on sideways cross-slopes exceeding 5 degrees due to immediate turnover rollover hazard.</div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 pt-1">The Tailgate Spreading Technique</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          When surfacing long aggregate driveways, experienced drivers can execute a <strong>tailgate spread</strong>. The driver chains the lower tailgate pins, allowing an opening of 3 to 5 inches, raises the hoist slightly, and drives forward in low gear at 3 to 5 mph. This deposits aggregate in an even 2-to-3-inch ribbon along the driveway centerline, cutting manual wheelbarrow and skid steer grading time by up to 75%. Tailgate spreading requires a straight path free from steep inclines or overhead obstacles.
        </p>
      </section>

