        </div>

        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          Examine three realistic project estimating proofs showing how commercial estimators calculate net volume, apply geometric adjustments, and account for delivery minimums:
        </p>

        <!-- Scenario A: Garage Slab with Footing -->
        <div class="p-5 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 space-y-3">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2 border-b border-slate-200 pb-2">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Scenario A: 24' × 24' × 4" Detached Garage Slab with Turned-Down Perimeter Footings</h3>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold bg-blue-100 text-blue-800 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full">Ready-Mix Concrete</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            A builder is framing a 24 ft by 24 ft garage. The design calls for a 4-inch reinforced slab with a monolithic turned-down edge footing around the entire 96-ft perimeter. The footing trench measures 12 inches wide and extends 8 inches below the slab (total 12 inches deep from the top of the pour).
          </p>
          <div class="bg-white p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 font-mono text-xs text-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
            <div><strong>Step 1: Main Slab Volume:</strong> 24 ft × 24 ft × (4 in ÷ 12) = 192 cu ft ÷ 27 = <span class="font-bold text-slate-900">7.11 cu yds</span></div>
            <div><strong>Step 2: Perimeter Footing Trench Volume:</strong> Perimeter = (24 + 24 + 24 + 24) = 96 linear ft. Footing width = 1.0 ft (12 in), Depth below slab = 0.667 ft (8 in). Volume = 96 ft × 1.0 ft × 0.667 ft = 64 cu ft ÷ 27 = <span class="font-bold text-slate-900">2.37 cu yds</span></div>
            <div><strong>Step 3: Net Geometric Concrete Volume:</strong> 7.11 + 2.37 = <span class="font-bold text-slate-900">9.48 cu yds</span></div>
            <div><strong>Step 4: Contractor Form-Flex & Subgrade Waste Allowance (8%):</strong> 9.48 cu yds × 1.08 = <span class="font-bold text-emerald-700">10.24 cu yds</span></div>
            <div class="pt-1 text-slate-600 font-sans"><strong>Dispatch Order:</strong> Ready-mix plants batch in 0.25-yard increments. Order exactly <strong class="text-slate-900 font-mono">10.25 cubic yards</strong> of 4,000 PSI concrete (one full 10-yard mixer truck + one 0.5-yard short-load run).</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Scenario B: Long Gravel Driveway -->
        <div class="p-5 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 space-y-3">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2 border-b border-slate-200 pb-2">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Scenario B: 120-Foot Country Driveway Installation (Multi-Layer Aggregate)</h3>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold bg-amber-100 text-amber-800 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full">Crushed Stone & Tonnage</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            Surfacing a 120 ft long by 10 ft wide private residential driveway. Design calls for a 6-inch compacted base of Crusher Run (Dense Graded Aggregate / CR6) topped with a 2-inch surface wearing course of #57 angular crushed limestone.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-white p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 font-mono text-xs text-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
            <div><strong>Step 1: Surface Area:</strong> 120 ft × 10 ft = 1,200 sq ft</div>
            <div><strong>Step 2: Base Layer Volume (6" Crusher Run):</strong> (1,200 sq ft × 6 in) ÷ 324 = 22.22 cu yds. Compaction allowance (+15%): 22.22 × 1.15 = <span class="font-bold text-slate-900">25.55 cu yds</span></div>
            <div><strong>Step 3: Base Tonnage (1.50 tons/yd³):</strong> 25.55 cu yds × 1.50 = <span class="font-bold text-emerald-700">38.33 Tons Crusher Run</span></div>
            <div><strong>Step 4: Surface Layer Volume (2" #57 Stone):</strong> (1,200 sq ft × 2 in) ÷ 324 = 7.41 cu yds. Compaction allowance (+8%): 7.41 × 1.08 = <span class="font-bold text-slate-900">8.00 cu yds</span></div>
            <div><strong>Step 5: Surface Tonnage (1.35 tons/yd³):</strong> 8.00 cu yds × 1.35 = <span class="font-bold text-emerald-700">10.80 Tons #57 Stone</span></div>
            <div class="pt-1 text-slate-600 font-sans"><strong>Dispatch Order:</strong> 2 Tri-Axle truckloads of Crusher Run (approx. 19.2 tons each) + 1 Tandem-Axle load of #57 Stone (10.8 tons).</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Scenario C: Lawn Grading & Topsoil Cap -->
        <div class="p-5 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 space-y-3">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2 border-b border-slate-200 pb-2">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Scenario C: Correcting Slope on an 1,800 Sq Ft Lawn with Fill Dirt & Topsoil</h3>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold bg-green-100 text-green-800 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full">Grading & Organic Soils</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            A landscaper is repairing a settling depression across an 1,800 sq ft backyard. The project requires an average of 4 inches of inorganic fill dirt to restore grade, topped with 2 inches of screened organic topsoil for new sod establishment.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-white p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 font-mono text-xs text-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
            <div><strong>Step 1: Fill Dirt Volume:</strong> (1,800 sq ft × 4 in) ÷ 324 = 22.22 cu yds. Natural settlement factor (+18%): 22.22 × 1.18 = <span class="font-bold text-slate-900">26.22 cu yds</span> (approx. 28.8 tons)</div>
            <div><strong>Step 2: Topsoil Cap Volume:</strong> (1,800 sq ft × 2 in) ÷ 324 = 11.11 cu yds. Fluff & settlement factor (+12%): 11.11 × 1.12 = <span class="font-bold text-slate-900">12.44 cu yds</span> (approx. 13.1 tons)</div>
            <div class="pt-1 text-slate-600 font-sans"><strong>Dispatch Order:</strong> Order 26.5 cubic yards of fill dirt (two tandem truckloads) and 12.5 cubic yards of screened topsoil (one tandem truckload).</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 7: Waste Allowance Matrix -->
      <section id="waste-matrix" class="bg-white p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-2xs space-y-5 scroll-mt-20">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-100 pb-4">
          <span class="text-[11px] font-bold text-emerald-700 bg-emerald-50 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md uppercase tracking-wider">Estimating Risk Management</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mt-2">Waste Allowance Matrix: Preventing Jobsite Material Shortages</h2>
        </div>

        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          A common mistake made by entry-level estimators is ordering exact net geometric volume. In physical construction, material is lost to substrate undulation, subgrade sinking, formwork bowing, wheelbarrow spillage, and perimeter cutting. Ordering exact volume leads to <strong>emergency short-load fees, cold joints in concrete, and idle labor hours</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-3.5">
          <div class="p-4 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-emerald-700 block">5% to 8%</span>
            <div class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 mt-1">Formed Concrete Slabs</div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 mt-1">Rigid form boards contain material cleanly. Waste accounts for 2x4 form deflection, screeding spillage, and pump hopper residue.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-blue-700 block">10% to 12%</span>
            <div class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 mt-1">Footings & Trenches</div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 mt-1">Excavated earth walls are naturally irregular. Backhoe bucket teeth leave scalloped edges that absorb additional volume.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-amber-700 block">12% to 15%</span>
            <div class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 mt-1">Gravel Sub-Bases</div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 mt-1">Heavy crushed stone embeds 0.5 to 1 inch directly into underlying soft clay or subsoil under vibratory plate compaction.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold text-purple-700 block">15% to 20%</span>
            <div class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 mt-1">Loose Dirt & Mulch</div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 mt-1">Fluffed organic material naturally settles and compresses upon watering and wind exposure. Edge raking results in minor dispersion.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
      <section id="faq" class="space-y-6 scroll-mt-20">
        <div>
          <span class="text-[11px] font-bold text-emerald-700 bg-emerald-50 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md uppercase tracking-wider">Trade Reference</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mt-1">Frequently Asked Questions: Volumetric & Material Estimation</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          {faqs.map((faq) => (
            <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 shadow-2xs">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm mb-2">{faq.question}</h3>
              <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">{faq.answer}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>
